tury  in fact this debate is a very modern one  the notion of the ascetic as a category  that underpinned the fine arts  did not exist until the 18th century it  emerged as the old commissioning culture  gave way  church and state replaced by the private  collector  craft relegated in status in deference  to fine art and the great academies  established with their hierarchies of  genre their exclusions of women and  workers  an era of transcendent value liberating  the artist  and indeed the viewer from the  responsibilities that shaped cultural  traditions  of centuries art history shaped and  instructed the story  of western art as a master narrative of  exploration and conquest  a story of pioneers just as compelling  as the narrative of colonial exploration  and conquest  that provided the backdrop to it art  history accounted  for the shape and content of our museums  and galleries  nurtured the markets and in turn forged  an enduring association  between money and artistic value but in  truth  no artist lives outside their time  however high the ivory tower  however white the cube they and their  work are intrinsically political  because both artists and work are  embedded in the ideological and cultural  context of their time  so the real question is not about  representation per se  although metaphor and analogy are  powerful tools of communication  tanya baguera the cuban activist artist  in a recent performance work entitled  tatlin's whisper  replaced kandinsky's cossacks with real  mounted police  who kettled the audience in the museum  space i often contrast brigara's work  with kandinsky's  what is at stake in her view is not so  much  representing what is political but  acting politically  and that is about the artist's duty to  think about the consequences of their  practice  that duty manifests itself in many  different forms  as british artist robin roberta smith  has observed  to draw a lemon or a landscape can be as  deeply subversive  at a time 